# REFERENCE for every setting, with DEVELOPMENT values. Do not copy this file to .env on a server:
# it turns sign-in checking off (AUTH_MODE=stub) and carries placeholder passwords. A real install
# gets its .env from the installer (scripts/talus-setup.sh), which generates every secret.
# For a development stack only: copy to .env and adjust as needed. .env is gitignored.
POSTGRES_DB=talusgis
POSTGRES_USER=talus
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme
# S-1a least-privilege runtime roles (decision-s1-least-privilege-db-role.md). The API's main pool
# uses talus_app (cannot tamper with audit); pg_tileserv uses talus_tiles (SELECT-only). The
# installer generates strong per-deployment values; db/init/00-roles.sh creates the roles at cluster init.
TALUS_APP_PASSWORD=changeme-talus-app
TALUS_TILES_PASSWORD=changeme-talus-tiles
# Dev-door publishes default to LOOPBACK in docker-compose.yml — Rider A,
# decision-talus-setup.md D4: the LAN sees only the edge (80/443). Two knobs per service:
#   *_PORT     = pure port NUMBER (also used by install/verify probe URLs and DSNs)
#   *_PUBLISH  = full compose publish spec, overrides the loopback bind
# e.g. POSTGRES_PORT=15432 moves the loopback port; POSTGRES_PUBLISH=0.0.0.0:5432
# deliberately re-exposes on all interfaces.
#POSTGRES_PORT=5432
#POSTGRES_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:5432

# api
# Loopback default (see the POSTGRES_PORT/PUBLISH note above); uncomment to override.
#API_PORT=8000
#API_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:8000
# stub: returns the validate-phase1 user without checking any token (dev only).
# keycloak: real JWT validation against the `talus` realm (KEYCLOAK_REALM below).
AUTH_MODE=stub

# Deployment environment (Talus 32 — web-edge hardening). development | staging | production.
# Set `production` on a real deployment: gates the interactive API docs (/docs, /redoc,
# /openapi.json) OFF, switches logs to JSON, and forbids stub auth. Leave development locally.
ENVIRONMENT=development

# --- Reverse proxy / TLS edge (Talus 32) — the `edge` compose profile -------------------------
# `docker compose --profile edge up` runs a hardened Caddy front: serves the built SPA (web/dist)
# + reverse-proxies the api under one TLS origin, and fronts Keycloak. Caddy auto-issues certs via
# ACME for public hostnames, or its INTERNAL CA for *.localhost (handy for local testing). Bare
# `docker compose up` (no profile) is unaffected — dev stays plain-HTTP localhost.
# PREREQUISITE: build the SPA first ->  npm --prefix web run build
TALUS_HOSTNAME=talus.localhost
KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME=auth.localhost
HTTP_PORT=80
HTTPS_PORT=443
# Behind this proxy, Caddy sets X-Forwarded-For — so set RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=true
# (below) when running the edge profile; it is the trusted proxy the anon limiter expects.

# Public / anonymous access (Talus 23) — deploy-posture master switch. Default OFF: the instance
# is NOT internet-facing, the "Public Access" share tier is rejected, and the anonymous read path
# is inert. Set true ONLY on a deliberately internet-exposed deployment.
PUBLIC_ANON_ACCESS_ENABLED=false

# Anonymous-surface rate limits (Talus 23) — per client IP, applied to ANONYMOUS traffic only.
# Portal (Bearer) sessions are never limited here. Tiles get a larger budget because a single
# public-map pan fires many tile requests. Defaults suit a typical public-facing municipal
# deployment; raise them for a high-traffic public site. A reverse proxy in front of the API is the
# production-grade defense; this app limiter is the turnkey default.
ANON_READ_RATE_PER_MIN=120
ANON_TILE_RATE_PER_MIN=600
# Anon full-set /export downloads (Open Data, Talus 33) — the heaviest per request, so a tight bucket.
ANON_DOWNLOAD_RATE_PER_MIN=10
# AUTHENTICATED tier — the ceiling for a desktop client (ArcGIS Pro / QGIS) authenticating with a
# personal access token. Per TOKEN, not per IP, so rotating IPs cannot amplify one credential. Only
# a token VALIDATED against the database reaches this tier; a junk credential stays on the anonymous
# bucket above. Raise for very heavy bulk editing; lower to tighten the bound on what a single leaked
# token could pull. This is NOT "unlimited" on purpose.
AUTH_RATE_PER_MIN=3000
# The FIRST request of a desktop session, before the token has been validated even once. Its own
# small per-IP bucket so that a shared address (a NAT'd office, or one under an anonymous flood) can
# still get one request through to prove its token real — without this, such clients could never
# reach the tier above at all. Also the bucket a PAT-shaped GUESS lands in, which is why it is small:
# it halves what credential probing gets today. Lower it to tighten probing further.
# Do NOT set this to 0 expecting "tiering off": as with the anon knobs above, 0 is a zero-capacity
# bucket that 429s EVERY desktop request, breaking ArcGIS Pro / QGIS sign-in outright. There is no
# off switch for tiering — RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false disables the whole limiter if that is what you want.
PAT_BOOTSTRAP_RATE_PER_MIN=60
# Open Data (Talus 33) — the publishing organization's name in the DCAT-US feed + schema.org JSON-LD
# (data.gov shows it). Set to your municipality's name; default is a harmless placeholder.
OPEN_DATA_PUBLISHER_NAME="Talus GIS"
# Trust X-Forwarded-For for the client IP ONLY behind a trusted reverse proxy that sets it — else a
# client could spoof its IP to evade the limit. Leave false when the API is directly exposed.
RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=false

# Browser origin(s) allowed to call the API from the portal (comma-separated).
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# Keycloak admin bootstrap (used on first boot to create the master-realm admin).
# Loopback default (see the POSTGRES_PORT/PUBLISH note above); uncomment to override.
#KEYCLOAK_PORT=8080
#KEYCLOAK_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:8080
# Optional hardening for an internet-facing server: answer the sign-in server's OWN admin console and
# master realm only to these addresses (space-separated addresses or CIDR ranges). List exact public
# IPv4 addresses; do not add private ranges on an internet-facing server (see proxy/Caddyfile for
# why). Unset = everyone, as before. Sign-in itself is never affected. Apply with:
#   docker compose up -d caddy
#KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_ALLOW_FROM="203.0.113.7 198.51.100.0/24"
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme-admin

# Keycloak's own Postgres backend (separate container, separate volume).
KEYCLOAK_DB_NAME=keycloak
KEYCLOAK_DB_USER=keycloak
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=changeme-kcdb

# The realm the api validates against and administers. EXPLICIT on purpose
# (decision-realm-rename.md §2): it used to be an invisible config.py default that
# 57 call sites silently agreed on, which is how a wrong assumption about it went
# unnoticed on every install. The api container reads this value. ★ The shell
# scripts under scripts/ read KEYCLOAK_REALM from the SHELL environment, not from
# this file, and fall back to the same default — export it if you override it here.
# install.sh re-asserts this key and the realm segment of the two URLs below on every run
# (fresh install or upgrade), so a .env born under an older realm name cannot leave the
# api validating against a realm Keycloak no longer serves.
KEYCLOAK_REALM=talus
# The api expects two URLs:
#  - KEYCLOAK_ISSUER is a string-equality check against the `iss` claim in the
#    token. Keycloak puts its public hostname (KC_HOSTNAME) in `iss`, so this
#    must match the public URL even when the api itself is in-network.
#  - KEYCLOAK_JWKS_URL is an actual HTTP fetch from inside the api container,
#    so it uses the internal docker service name.
KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080/realms/talus
KEYCLOAK_AUDIENCE=talus-api
KEYCLOAK_JWKS_URL=http://keycloak:8080/realms/talus/protocol/openid-connect/certs

# Secret of the `talus-api` Keycloak client. scripts/render-realm.sh injects this value
# into the rendered realm (keycloak/import/<realm>-realm.json, gitignored) — the realm and .env
# can no longer drift apart. The verify scripts read it from here and FAIL LOUD if unset
# (A2: the old not-for-production fallbacks are gone). Clean installs generate it
# (scripts/generate-secrets.sh); the value below is the dev convention.
TALUS_API_CLIENT_SECRET=dev-talus-api-secret-not-for-production

# Talus 19 — demo service-account client secret. The `talus-sa-demo` client is DEV-TREE-ONLY
# surface (decision-deployment-appliance.md §5.4, decision-demo-seed-retirement.md §1): not in
# the realm template, and created only by the dev tree's bootstrap-19-service-accounts.sh,
# which does not ship. A Talus install never creates this client; the key is kept so the two
# trees share one .env shape.
TALUS_SA_DEMO_CLIENT_SECRET=dev-talus-sa-demo-secret-not-for-production

# OpenFGA (Layer 7b) — per-layer authorization. Internal-only service (no host
# port). Its own Postgres datastore: separate container + volume from app/keycloak.
OPENFGA_DB_NAME=openfga
OPENFGA_DB_USER=openfga
OPENFGA_DB_PASSWORD=changeme-openfgadb
# The api reaches OpenFGA over the compose network at this URL. STORE_ID and
# MODEL_ID are written by scripts/load-openfga-model.sh after the model loads —
# leave them blank here; they are filled into your local .env, not committed.
OPENFGA_API_URL=http://openfga:8080
OPENFGA_STORE_ID=
OPENFGA_MODEL_ID=

# Storage (Talus 16 / object-store slice) — media, rasters, and the local backup
# default are plain files on the `storagedata` Docker volume, mounted in the api at
# STORAGE_ROOT. No object-store server, no storage credentials. The api /media proxy
# is the only way in (bytes are never browser-reachable). The *_BUCKET names are the
# subtree names under the root — a sysadmin can `ls` them.
STORAGE_ROOT=/storage
MEDIA_BUCKET=talus-media

# Disaster recovery — backup / retention / restore (Talus 40). A scheduled pg_dump -Fc +
# an incremental copy of the media bucket(s) into object storage, run by the in-app
# scheduler (the T39 seam). Restore is a documented runbook (docs/dr-restore-runbook.md),
# never an in-product button. All have safe defaults — you only need to set these to tune
# cadence/retention or (importantly) to send backups OFF-HOST.
BACKUP_ENABLED=true
BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400      # daily
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30           # prune dump artifacts older than this...
BACKUP_RETENTION_MIN_KEEP=7        # ...but ALWAYS keep at least this many most-recent (the floor)
BACKUP_BUCKET=talus-backups        # the backups subtree under STORAGE_ROOT (or the off-host S3 bucket)
BACKUP_OBJECT_BUCKETS=             # EMPTY = derive [MEDIA_BUCKET, RASTER_BUCKET] (recommended); set only to override
# >>> THE REAL DR KNOB <<< By default backups land on the SAME disk as your live data, which
# protects against bad migrations / app bugs / accidental deletes but NOT host loss. For genuine
# disaster recovery, point these at off-host S3 (AWS S3, Backblaze B2, Wasabi, a NAS's S3 door).
# Leave all three empty for the local default. Set ALL THREE to go off-host (no code change);
# a partial config errors loudly rather than silently writing to the local disk.
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# Client-side encryption (Talus 40.1) — comma-separated `age` PUBLIC keys. Empty = OFF.
# When set, every backup artifact (dump + media) is age-encrypted BEFORE upload, so an
# off-host/cloud target never holds plaintext. ASYMMETRIC: the api holds only the public
# key and CANNOT decrypt its own backups — keep the matching PRIVATE key OFF-HOST (a safe /
# password manager); it's needed only at restore. List ≥2 recipients (primary + break-glass)
# so a single lost key isn't lost backups. Generate with:  age-keygen -o key.txt
#   BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS=age1q<primary...>,age1q<breakglass...>
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS=
# Full-system DR (Talus 42) — Talus is a THREE-key system: Postgres (data) + Keycloak
# (identity) + OpenFGA (authorization), each its own database. A backup dumps ALL THREE,
# so a restore brings back logins + the sharing graph, not just GIS data. These default
# (in docker-compose.yml) to the internal Keycloak / OpenFGA Postgres — leave them unset
# for turnkey full backups. Set one to EMPTY to SKIP it (e.g. you run identity/authz
# externally) — that yields an honest "talusgis + media only" backup, not an error.
#   BACKUP_KEYCLOAK_DB_URL=        # empty = skip backing up the Keycloak (identity) DB
#   BACKUP_OPENFGA_DB_URL=         # empty = skip backing up the OpenFGA (authz) DB
# NOTE: your .env itself is NEVER backed up (secrets must not ride with the data). It is
# the "master key" for a restore (OpenFGA STORE/MODEL IDs, DB passwords) — keep it safe
# separately. See docs/dr-restore-runbook.md + docs/dr-hardening.md.

# Secrets-at-rest key (decision-geocoder-token-locators.md) — the ONE Fernet key the api uses
# to encrypt credentials an admin stores through the UI (first consumer: geocoder tokens).
# The key lives HERE, the ciphertext in the DB — neither alone is the secret, so a DB dump or
# backup never contains a usable credential. Generate once per deployment:
#   python3 -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
# Unset is fine until an admin stores a token (the UI then shows an actionable error).
# LOSING the key = stored tokens become "credential unavailable"; re-enter them in the UI
# (see docs/dr-restore-runbook.md). Keep it with the rest of this .env (the restore master key).
# TALUS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Geocoding / address search (Talus 18c) — external locator services Talus *consumes*.
# Talus never builds or operates a geocoder; this is just connection info for a locator
# the deployment already runs.
# >>> PREFER THE UI: Admin → Geocoders manages locators in-portal — INCLUDING token-secured
# ones (the token is stored encrypted; see TALUS_ENCRYPTION_KEY above). This env var remains
# as a bootstrap/back-compat path; entries here are honored via a union with the DB list.
# GEOCODERS is a JSON array of service definitions:
#   {name, type, url, out_sr?, token?, order?, extent?}
#     name   unique id, surfaced to the client as the result `source` + the "Search in" picker
#     type   "arcgis" (ArcGIS REST GeocodeServer) | "nominatim" (OSS)
#     url    base service URL (GeocodeServer root, or the Nominatim base)
#     out_sr SR requested from the service — leave at 4326 (v1 does not reproject)
#     token  static ArcGIS token for a secured service (proxy-injected; omit for public)
#     order  ordered-list fallback — lower is tried first (point locator before street)
#     extent [xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax] EPSG:4326 — restrict results to this box (e.g. the county),
#            so "100 Main" doesn't match worldwide. Authoritative; wins over the map viewport.
# Unset/empty => only the locators kept in the portal are used. A new install starts with one
# there (the public OpenStreetMap geocoder); with none at all the search returns empty and the
# map still works.
# Two+ locators => the map shows a "Search in" picker (All / each); one => no picker.
#
# Typical county setup — the point locator first, the street locator as fallback.
# NOTE: many agency locators are TOKEN-SECURED and answer HTTP 200 with a code-499
# "Token Required" body rather than a 401; supply a static `token` per entry or search
# fails soft (logged, empty result). Substitute your own ArcGIS Server host below.
# GEOCODERS=[{"name":"county-point","type":"arcgis","url":"https://gis.example.gov/server/rest/services/Locators/WebPointLocator/GeocodeServer","token":"<arcgis-token>","order":1},{"name":"county-street","type":"arcgis","url":"https://gis.example.gov/server/rest/services/Locators/WebStreetLocator/GeocodeServer","token":"<arcgis-token>","order":2}]
#
# OpenStreetMap Nominatim (OSS) — worldwide, token-free; the air-gapped path is to point `url`
# at a SELF-HOSTED instance. The public instance below rate-limits (~1 req/s).
# Add `extent` to keep results local — e.g. a county-sized box: "extent":[WEST,SOUTH,EAST,NORTH] in longitude/latitude degrees
# GEOCODERS=[{"name":"osm","type":"nominatim","url":"https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org","order":1}]
